The simplest approach is a **custom field** (not an entity extension), since merchants can edit it in the Administration and it only needs scalar values. The docs I found cover the field definition and the storefront template, but not the step that attaches the set to the product entity, so you'll need to check that part yourself.

**1. Register the custom field set in your plugin**

Create the set and field from a lifecycle method (for example `install()`), using the `custom_field_set.repository` service. The docs show this pattern [11]:

```php
use Shopware\Core\System\CustomField\CustomFieldTypes;
use \Shopware\Core\Defaults;

[...]

$this->customFieldSetRepository->create([
    [
        'name' => 'swag_example_set',
        'global' => true,
        'config' => [
            'label' => [
                'en-GB' => 'English custom field set label',
                'de-DE' => 'German custom field set label',
                Defaults::LANGUAGE_SYSTEM => "Mention the fallback label here"
            ]
        ],
        'customFields' => [
            [
                'name' => 'swag_example_size',
                'type' => CustomFieldTypes::INT,
                'includeInSearch' => true,
                'config' => [
                    'label' => [
                        'en-GB' => 'English custom field label',
                        'de-DE' => 'German custom field label',
                        Defaults::LANGUAGE_SYSTEM => "Mention the fallback label here"
                    ],
                    'customFieldPosition' => 1
                ]
            ]
        ]
    ]
], $context);
```

For a text field like "material", use the string type instead of `CustomFieldTypes::INT`. I did not confirm the exact constant name for strings, so check `CustomFieldTypes` in the Shopware source. The docs also say that for merchants to edit and delete the set in the Administration, `global` must be `false` [11]. Note that the `global` value in the example is `true`.

Alternatively, from Shopware 6.7.13.0 you can declare sets in `Resources/config/custom-fields.xml` under `src`. Shopware then creates, updates and removes them on install, update and uninstall [14][15].

Remove the set on uninstall, using the repository's standard methods [12].

**2. Show the value on the product detail page**

Extend the Storefront template and read the value from the product. The docs give this example for the product description block [6]:

```twig
// <plugin root>/src/Resources/views/storefront/page/product-detail/description.html.twig
{% sw_extends '@Storefront/storefront/page/product-detail/description.html.twig' %}

{% block page_product_detail_description_content_text %}
    {{ parent() }}

    {# Insert your custom field here, as seen below: #}
    {{ "customFields.my_test_field"|trans|sw_sanitize }}: {{ page.product.translated.customFields.my_test_field }}
{% endblock %}
```

Replace `my_test_field` with your field name (for example `swag_material`). The docs use the description block as their example, so you may want a different block for a material display.

**What's missing from the docs I found:** how to link the custom field set to the `product` entity. Without that link the field may not appear on products in the Administration. Check the full "Add custom fields to an entity" section of the custom field guide for the relation step.

To run a task every 10 minutes that only works when a plugin checkbox is enabled, combine a scheduled task with a boolean config field.

**1. Add the checkbox to `config.xml`**

Use a `bool` input field. The example config shows the `type="bool"` syntax [8]:

```xml
<!-- PLUGIN_ROOT/src/Resources/config/config.xml -->
<card>
    <title>Basic Configuration</title>
    <input-field type="bool">
        <name>enableCleanup</name>
        <defaultValue>false</defaultValue>
        <label>Enable cleanup task</label>
    </input-field>
</card>
```

After saving, refresh the Administration and open the plugin's **Configure** action to set the value [8].

**2. Create the task class (600 seconds = 10 minutes)**

`getDefaultInterval()` takes seconds [5]:

```php
// PLUGIN_ROOT/src/Service/ScheduledTask/ExampleTask.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Swag\BasicExample\Service\ScheduledTask;

use Shopware\Core\Framework\MessageQueue\ScheduledTask\ScheduledTask;

class ExampleTask extends ScheduledTask
{
    public static function getTaskName(): string
    {
        return 'swag.example_task';
    }

    public static function getDefaultInterval(): int
    {
        return 600; // 10 minutes
    }
}
```

The task name should carry a vendor prefix to avoid collisions [5]. Replace `Swag\BasicExample` with your own namespace.

**3. Create the handler and check the setting in `run()`**

The handler is the Messenger consumer that executes `run()` [5]. Inject `SystemConfigService` and read the prefixed key `<BundleName>.config.<configName>` [6]:

```php
// PLUGIN_ROOT/src/Service/ScheduledTask/ExampleTaskHandler.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Swag\BasicExample\Service\ScheduledTask;

use Shopware\Core\Framework\MessageQueue\ScheduledTask\ScheduledTaskHandler;
use Shopware\Core\System\SystemConfig\SystemConfigService;
use Symfony\Component\Messenger\Attribute\AsMessageHandler;

#[AsMessageHandler(handles: ExampleTask::class)]
class ExampleTaskHandler extends ScheduledTaskHandler
{
    private SystemConfigService $systemConfigService;

    public function __construct(
        /* inject the repository from your constructor here, as required by ScheduledTaskHandler */
        SystemConfigService $systemConfigService
    ) {
        $this->systemConfigService = $systemConfigService;
    }

    public function run(): void
    {
        // Null = global value; pass a sales channel ID to read a sales-channel-specific value [6]
        $enabled = $this->systemConfigService->get('SwagBasicExample.config.enableCleanup');

        if (!$enabled) {
            return;
        }

        //... your task logic
    }
}
```

Important: the handler's constructor must also pass the arguments that `ScheduledTaskHandler` needs. The docs I retrieved don't show that constructor, so check the base class signature. The `SwagBasicExample` prefix assumes your plugin's bundle name, so adjust it to match yours.

**4. Register task and handler in `services.php`**

The docs say `services.php` references both the task and its handler from `Service/ScheduledTask` [5], but the retrieved sources don't include the exact registration snippet. Check the "Add Scheduled Task" guide for the tag and the registration code.

**Optional: test it**

Run a single task without waiting for the queue with `bin/console scheduled-task:run-single <task-name>` (available from Shopware 6.7.2.0) [4]. For example: `bin/console scheduled-task:run-single swag.example_task`.

I could not find the full plugin wiring in the documentation, so this answer covers the route class and the rate limiter, and notes what is missing.

**1. Create the route class**

The Store API guide builds a route `ExampleRoute` under `/store-api/example`. It is an `AbstractExampleRoute` subclass with a `#[Route]` attribute and the Store API route scope [4]. Adapt it like this:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Core/Content/Example/SalesChannel/ExampleRoute.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Swag\BasicExample\Core\Content\Example\SalesChannel;

use Shopware\Core\Framework\Routing\StoreApiRouteScope;
use Shopware\Core\PlatformRequest;
use Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\EntityRepository;
use Shopware\Core\Framework\Plugin\Exception\DecorationPatternException;
use Shopware\Core\System\SalesChannel\SalesChannelContext;
use Symfony\Component\Routing\Attribute\Route;

#[Route(defaults: [PlatformRequest::ATTRIBUTE_ROUTE_SCOPE => [StoreApiRouteScope::ID]])]
class ExampleRoute extends AbstractExampleRoute
{
    // constructor injection of services (including RateLimiter, see step 3)

    public function getDecorated(): AbstractExampleRoute
    {
        throw new DecorationPatternException(self::class);
    }

    #[Route(path: '/store-api/example', name: 'store-api.example.search', methods: ['GET','POST'])]
    public function load(Request $request, SalesChannelContext $context): ExampleRouteResponse
    {
        // rate limit goes here (step 3)
        //... your logic, return ExampleRouteResponse
    }
}
```

The `getDecorated()` method must throw `DecorationPatternException` because the route has no decoration [4]. The guide's example uses a `Criteria` parameter and a repository search. Your endpoint can take a `Request` instead, as shown in the rate limit example [6]. Its exact signature and the `ExampleRouteResponse` class are up to you.

The guide also mentions a service definition that registers the route with the DI container, and a `routes.php` that imports it for discovery [4]. The documentation does not show their contents, so check the Store API guide for those files. You can verify the route with `./bin/console debug:router store-api.example.search` [3].

**2. Configure the rate limiter**

Create `<plugin root>/src/Resources/config/rate_limiter.yaml`. The root key is a unique name, here `example_route` [8]:

```yaml
// <plugin root>/src/Resources/config/rate_limiter.yaml
example_route:
    enabled: true
    policy: 'time_backoff'
```

Supported policies are `fixed_window`, `sliding_window`, `token_bucket`, `time_backoff` and `system_config` [8]. The `time_backoff` policy has its own configuration described in the rate limiter hosting guide [8]. I could not retrieve those keys, and the Symfony keys for the other policies are not in the sources I read.

**3. Inject the limiter and call it per IP**

Inject `Shopware\Core\Framework\RateLimiter\RateLimiter` into the constructor [7]:

```php
use Shopware\Core\Framework\RateLimiter\RateLimiter;

public function __construct(private RateLimiter $rateLimiter) {}
```

Then call `ensureAccepted` at the start of the route method, passing the route name from the YAML and the client IP [7]:

```php
#[Route(path: '/store-api/example', name: 'store-api.example.search', methods: ['GET','POST'])]
public function load(Request $request, SalesChannelContext $context): ExampleRouteResponse
{
    $this->rateLimiter->ensureAccepted('example_route', $request->getClientIp());

    //... your logic
}
```

If the limit is exceeded, it throws `Shopware\Core\Framework\RateLimiter\Exception\RateLimitExceededException` [7].

To scope the limit per sales channel, pass the sales channel ID as the third argument and include it in the key. This needs Shopware 6.7.16.0 or later, and before 6.8.0.0 it can only be passed positionally [6]:

```php
$key = $request->getClientIp(). '-'. $context->getSalesChannelId();
$this->rateLimiter->ensureAccepted('example_route', $key, $context->getSalesChannelId());
```

Call `reset()` with the same key after a successful request, if you want successful calls to clear the counter [6]. For brute-force protection on a login-style endpoint, you would typically count every attempt and not reset it.

**Not covered by the sources:**

Shopware's message queue is built on Symfony Messenger. You need three parts: a message class, a dispatch call, and a handler. I could not read the full handler guide, so step 3 is only partly covered.

**1. Define the message**

Create the class in `<plugin root>/src/MessageQueue/Message`. Implementing `AsyncMessageInterface` makes it asynchronous; by default messages are handled synchronously [14].

```php
// <plugin root>/src/MessageQueue/Message/SmsNotification.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Swag\BasicExample\MessageQueue\Message;

use Shopware\Core\Framework\MessageQueue\AsyncMessageInterface;

class SmsNotification implements AsyncMessageInterface
{
    private string $content;

    public function __construct(string $content)
    {
        $this->content = $content;
    }

    public function getContent(): string
    {
        return $this->content;
    }
}
```
[14]

The message should be serializable and contain everything the handler needs [3]. Pass the phone number and text, not objects such as entities.

**2. Dispatch it**

Inject `Symfony\Component\Messenger\MessageBusInterface`, which dispatches through the `messenger.default_bus` bus [13]:

```php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Swag\BasicExample\Service;

use Swag\BasicExample\MessageQueue\Message\SmsNotification;
use Symfony\Component\Messenger\MessageBusInterface;

class ExampleSender
{
    private MessageBusInterface $bus;

    public function __construct(MessageBusInterface $bus)
    {
        $this->bus = $bus;
    }

    public function sendMessage(string $message): void
    {
        $this->bus->dispatch(new SmsNotification($message));
    }
}
```
[13]

Use this from your plugin's SMS code path. The request then only queues the message and returns.

**3. Write the handler**

The handler guide says to create a class (the example is named `SmsHandler`), mark it with the PHP attribute `#[AsMessageHandler]`, and implement `__invoke`. It also says to register the handler with the `messenger.message_handler` tag [9]. I could not read the full handler code, so check the guide for the exact class body and service registration: https://developer.shopware.com/docs/guides/plugins/plugins/framework/message-queue/add-message-handler.html

**4. Run the consumer**

Messages are not processed during the request. A worker has to consume them. The CLI worker is the recommended method [4]:

```bash
bin/console messenger:consume async --time-limit=60 --memory-limit=128M
```
[4]

On a production system, process the queue with CLI processes rather than the Administration worker [7].

**Optional: low priority.** If the SMS does not need to go out immediately, implement `Shopware\Core\Framework\MessageQueue\LowPriorityMessageInterface` instead of `AsyncMessageInterface`. This uses the `low_priority` queue, which exists only in Shopware 6.5.7.0 and later [3][1].

Use a `OneToManyAssociationField` on the brand side and a `FkField` plus `ManyToOneAssociationField` on the item side. The documentation's example uses `bar`/`foo`, which maps to your `brand`/`item` as follows.

**1. Brand definition** (`BrandDefinition.php`)

```php
protected function defineFields(): FieldCollection
{
    return new FieldCollection([
        (new IdField('id', 'id'))->addFlags(new Required(), new PrimaryKey()),

        new OneToManyAssociationField('items', ItemDefinition::class, 'brand_id')
    ]);
}
```

The parameters are the property name (`items`), the class of the referenced definition, and the column in the item table that points back to the brand. [1]

**2. Item definition** (`ItemDefinition.php`)

```php
protected function defineFields(): FieldCollection
{
    return new FieldCollection([
        (new IdField('id', 'id'))->addFlags(new Required(), new PrimaryKey()),
        (new FkField('brand_id', 'brandId', BrandDefinition::class))->addFlags(new Required()),

        new ManyToOneAssociationField('brand', 'brand_id', BrandDefinition::class, 'id'),
    ]);
}
```

The `FkField` takes the snake_case column name, the camelCase property name, and the referenced definition. The `ManyToOneAssociationField` takes the property name (`brand`), the FK column, the referenced definition, and the referenced ID column. [1]

The `swag_brand` and `swag_item` tables and the `brand_id` column still need to exist in your database, which the documentation doesn't cover in this part. [1]

**3. Load a brand with its items**

```php
public function readData(Context $context): void
{
    $criteria = new Criteria();
    $criteria->addFilter(new EqualsFilter('name', 'Example name'));
    $criteria->addAssociation('items');

    $brands = $this->brandRepository->search($criteria, $context);
}
```

Pass the association's property name (`items`) to `addAssociation()`. [7]

To load only some items, use `getAssociation()`, which returns a nested `Criteria` you can filter:

```php
$criteria->getAssociation('items')->addFilter(new RangeFilter('points', [
    RangeFilter::GTE => 4
]));
```

[7]

The documentation's example filters on `points`, so replace that with a field on your item entity. [7]

Here's how to set it up, based on the Shopware guides for custom storefront controllers and HTTP caching.

**1. Create the controller**

Extend `StorefrontController`, set the storefront route scope on the class, and add the `/example` route with `_httpCache` set to `true` on the method:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Storefront/Controller/ExampleController.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Swag\BasicExample\Storefront\Controller;

use Shopware\Core\PlatformRequest;
use Shopware\Core\System\SalesChannel\SalesChannelContext;
use Shopware\Storefront\Controller\StorefrontController;
use Shopware\Storefront\Framework\Routing\StorefrontRouteScope;
use Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Request;
use Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Response;
use Symfony\Component\Routing\Attribute\Route;

#[Route(defaults: [PlatformRequest::ATTRIBUTE_ROUTE_SCOPE => [StorefrontRouteScope::ID]])]
class ExampleController extends StorefrontController
{

    #[Route(path: '/example', name: 'frontend.example.example', methods: ['GET'], defaults: ['_httpCache' => true])]
    public function showExample(): Response
    {
        return $this->renderStorefront('@SwagBasicExample/storefront/page/example/index.html.twig', [
            'example' => 'Hello world'
        ]);
    }
}
```
[1]

Notes on the code:
- The class must extend `StorefrontController` and define the storefront route scope [7][9].
- The route name `frontend.example.example` uses the `frontend` prefix, which identifies it as a Storefront route [7].
- `renderStorefront` renders the Twig template and returns a `Response` [7].

**2. Make it cacheable**

Setting `defaults: ['_httpCache' => true]` on the route attribute makes the response cacheable. Once this is set, the core handles the rest. If the route is called several times in the same state, only the first request generates a response and later requests get the same one [1].

Shopware distinguishes two user states that the HTTP cache reacts to: `logged-in` (user is logged in) and `cart-filled` (products are in the cart) [1]. Responses for those states are not cached in the usual way, so a page that must be shared across visitors should be designed around that.

**3. Register the controller as a service**

A controller is a service, so it must be registered in the service container [8]. The sources I retrieved did not include the exact service XML/YAML, so check the "Adding custom Storefront controller" guide for that step.

**Caveats**
- The sources do not show how to define custom TTLs or per-route caching policies beyond `_httpCache`. The caching policy configuration (`CACHE_REWORK` feature flag, which is experimental) is described in the caching guide [4], and I did not read it in full, so I can't give you its exact settings.
- Cache invalidation is handled by the core through cache tags, and Storefront routes follow the invalidation of the corresponding Store API routes [5].

To add the module to the menu and restrict it by role, you need three parts: the menu entry, the ACL privilege, and the route protection. I confirmed the first two from the docs. For the menu-entry restriction I could not read the exact property, so that step is incomplete (see below).

**1. Add the menu entry**

Put `navigation` in your module registration. Plugin modules can't add top-level entries, so set `parent` to an existing category. The entry also needs an `id`:

```javascript
// <plugin root>/src/Resources/app/administration/src/module/swag-example/index.js
navigation: [{
    id: 'swag-custommodule-list',
    label: 'CustomModule',
    color: '#ff3d58',
    path: 'swag.custommodule.list',
    icon: 'default-shopping-paper-bag-product',
    parent: 'sw-catalogue',
    position: 100
}]
```
[1]

**2. Register the privilege**

Register the privilege with the privilege service. Use the recommended pattern of an `acl` folder imported from your component's `index.js`:

```javascript
// <plugin root>/src/Resources/app/administration/src/<your-component>/acl/index.js
Shopware.Service('privileges').addPrivilegeMappingEntry({
    category: 'permissions',
    parent: null,
    key: 'your_key',
    roles: {
        viewer: {
            privileges: [],
            dependencies: []
        },
        editor: {
            privileges: [],
            dependencies: []
        },
        creator: {
            privileges: [],
            dependencies: []
        },
        deleter: {
            privileges: [],
            dependencies: []
        }
    }
});
```
[14]

Add the entity operations each role needs to `privileges`, in the form `entity_name:operation`, e.g. `product:read`. Use `dependencies` to require another role, e.g. `review.viewer`. [13] The privilege must be registered before the user opens the roles detail page. [14] Registered privileges then appear under the roles in Users & Permissions. [13]

**3. Protect the routes**

Add `privilege` to the `meta` of each route. This restricts the pages, so users without the privilege can't open them:

```javascript
Module.register('your-plugin-module', {
    routes: {
        detail: {
            component: 'your-plugin-detail',
            path: 'your-plugin',
            meta: {
                privilege: 'your_key.your_role' // e.g. 'product.viewer'
            }
        }
    }
});
```
[11]

**4. Menu entry visibility (not fully confirmed)**

The ACL guide lists "protect your menu entries" as a topic, but I could not read that section before my tool budget ran out. Check the "Adding Permissions" page of the Shopware developer docs for the exact property to put on the `navigation` entry. I haven't verified its name, so don't assume it is `privilege`.

**Note:** ACL rules only apply in the Administration. Users can still reach the backend through direct API calls, so the server-side API permissions remain the real security boundary. [7]

The full path has three parts: a Administration element, a Storefront Twig template, and a server-side data resolver. I didn't retrieve the docs' Step 1–2 (where the element is declared and its `defaultConfig` is set), so check that step in the guide before you build.

**1. Administration: register the element**

Create the preview, main component, and config component as JS components. The docs use this pattern:

- **Preview** (block picker thumbnail): `Shopware.Component.register('cms-el-preview-dailymotion',...)` [9]
- **Main component** (editor view): register `cms-el-dailymotion` with the `cms-element` mixin and call `this.initElementConfig('dailymotion')` in `created()`. Read the value through `element.config.url.value` [9]
- **Config component** (modal form): register `cms-el-config-dailymotion` with an `mt-text-field` bound to `element.config.url.value`, so the merchant can enter the video ID [9]

Use the Meteor components for the UI [9]. The docs example uses the element name `dailymotion`.

**2. Storefront: render the element**

Place the template at `<plugin root>/src/Resources/views/storefront/element/cms-element-dailymotion.html.twig`. The prefix `cms-element-` is required, and the technical name must match the element name [6]. The docs example reads the configured value directly:

```twig
<div class="cms-element-dailymotion">
    <iframe
        src="https://www.dailymotion.com/embed/video/{{ element.config.url.value }}"
        frameborder="0"
        type="text/html"
        width="100%"
        height="480"
    />
</div>
```

`element` is passed to the template automatically and holds the meta data and config values [6]. Clear the Storefront cache after adding the template [6].

**3. Server side: load extra data with a resolver**

Use a resolver when the element needs more than plain config values, such as entities or external API data [10].

Create a class that extends `AbstractCmsElementResolver`. It must implement `getType()`, `collect()` and `enrich()` [10]:

```php
namespace Swag\BasicExample\DataResolver;

use Shopware\Core\Content\Cms\Aggregate\CmsSlot\CmsSlotEntity;
use Shopware\Core\Content\Cms\DataResolver\Element\AbstractCmsElementResolver;
use Shopware\Core\Content\Cms\DataResolver\Element\ElementDataCollection;
use Shopware\Core\Content\Cms\DataResolver\ResolverContext\ResolverContext;
use Shopware\Core\Content\Cms\DataResolver\CriteriaCollection;

class DailyMotionCmsElementResolver extends AbstractCmsElementResolver
{
    public function getType(): string
    {
        return 'dailymotion';
    }

    public function collect(CmsSlotEntity $slot, ResolverContext $resolverContext): ?CriteriaCollection
    {
        return null;
    }

    public function enrich(CmsSlotEntity $slot, ResolverContext $resolverContext, ElementDataCollection $result): void
    {

    }
}
```
[10]

- `getType()` returns the element name, and the resolver runs for every element of that type [10].
- `collect()` builds the criteria. Read config with `$slot->getFieldConfig()` and `$config->get('fieldName')`. If an entity ID is stored there, add a `Criteria` to a `CriteriaCollection` [12].
- `enrich()` processes the resolved data. It can also call an external API with a config value, then store the result with `$slot->setData()` [12].

Register the resolver in `<plugin root>/src/Resources/config/services.php` with the `shopware.cms.data_resolver` tag [10]:

```php
use Swag\BasicExample\DataResolver\DailyMotionCmsElementResolver;
use Symfony\Component\DependencyInjection\Loader\Configurator\ContainerConfigurator;

return static function (ContainerConfigurator $configurator): void {
    $services = $configurator->services();

    $services->set(DailyMotionCmsElementResolver::class)
        ->tag('shopware.cms.data_resolver');
};
```
[10]

For a plain video ID you may not need a resolver at all: the Storefront template can read `element.config.url.value` directly [6]. Use a resolver if you need to load or fetch data on the server before rendering.

**Creating the payment handler**

Create a class that extends `AbstractPaymentHandler` and implements `supports()` and `pay()`. For a synchronous payment, `pay()` returns `null`, and no `finalize()` method is needed [1]:

```php
class MyCustomPaymentHandler extends AbstractPaymentHandler
{
    public function __construct(private readonly OrderTransactionStateHandler $transactionStateHandler)
    {
    }

    public function supports(PaymentHandlerType $type, string $paymentMethodId, Context $context): bool
    {
        // This payment handler does not support recurring payments nor refunds
        return false;
    }

    public function pay(Request $request, PaymentTransactionStruct $transaction, Context $context, ?Struct $validateStruct): ?RedirectResponse
    {
        // In here you should probably call your payment provider to process the payment
        // $this->myPaymentProvider->processPayment($transaction);

        // afterward you should update the transaction with the new state
        $this->transactionStateHandler->process($transaction->getOrderTransactionId(), $context);

        return null;
    }
}
```
[1]

If the payment provider call can fail, throw a `PaymentException`. Shopware then sets the transaction to the `failed` state [1].

**Changing the transaction status**

- The simplest approach is to inject `OrderTransactionStateHandler` and call its `process()` method with the transaction ID, as in the example above [1]. The documentation I found does not show a `paid` method on this handler, so I can't confirm that it exists.
- For a general state change, inject `Shopware\Core\System\StateMachine\StateMachineRegistry` and call `transition()` with a `Transition` object and the `Context`. The `Transition` takes four arguments: the entity name (use the definition constant, e.g. `order_transaction`), the entity ID, the transition name, and the state field name (`stateId`) [6][9].

**Checking the possible status changes**

Call `getAvailableTransitions` on the `StateMachineRegistry`. It takes the entity name, the entity ID, the state field (`'stateId'`) and the context, and returns the transitions allowed from the current state [11]:

```php
$transitions = $this->stateMachineRegistry->getAvailableTransitions(
    OrderDefinition::ENTITY_NAME,
    '<Order ID here>',
    'stateId',
    $context
);
```
[11]

For the order transaction, pass the transaction's entity name and ID instead. The documentation example uses the order entity.

**Gaps:** I could not retrieve the list of order transaction transition names (such as the one that leads to "paid"). Check them with `bin/console state-machine:dump order_transaction.state`, which exports the registered states and transitions, including those added by extensions [10].

I can cover the command setup and the upsert pattern from the docs. The docs don't include a complete import example, so the code below is a sketch that combines those pieces.

**1. Create the command class**

Shopware commands are Symfony Console commands. Put the class in your plugin, for example `src/Command/ImportProductsCommand.php` [1]:

```php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Swag\BasicExample\Command;

use Symfony\Component\Console\Attribute\AsCommand;
use Symfony\Component\Console\Command\Command;
use Symfony\Component\Console\Input\InputInterface;
use Symfony\Component\Console\Output\OutputInterface;

#[AsCommand(name: 'swag:product:import', description: 'Imports products')]
class ImportProductsCommand extends Command
{
    protected function execute(InputInterface $input, OutputInterface $output): int
    {
        $output->writeln('Importing products');

        return Command::SUCCESS;
    }
}
```

**2. Register it as a service with the `console.command` tag**

In `src/Resources/config/services.php` [1]:

```php
$services->set(Swag\BasicExample\Command\ImportProductsCommand::class)
    ->tag('console.command');
```

The docs say commands registered this way are automatically available via `bin/console` [1]. The docs don't show how to inject the product repository into the command. Inject it through the service definition's constructor arguments, as you would for any service.

**3. Upsert the product**

The `upsert()` method creates or updates entities. If the payload contains an `id` that already exists, the entity is updated. Otherwise it is created [6]. The docs also say that without an `id`, data is always created and never updated [7].

So the command needs to find the existing product's ID first. The docs show this pattern with `searchIds()` and `firstId()` plus an `EqualsFilter` [7][11]. Filtering by `productNumber` instead of `name` is my adaptation:

```php
use Shopware\Core\Framework\Context;
use Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\EntityRepository;
use Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\Search\Criteria;
use Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\Search\Filter\EqualsFilter;

// inside a method that has $context and the injected $productRepository
$criteria = new Criteria();
$criteria->addFilter(new EqualsFilter('productNumber', $row['productNumber']));

$productId = $this->productRepository->searchIds($criteria, $context)->firstId();

$this->productRepository->upsert([
    [
        'id' => $productId ?? Uuid::randomHex(),
        'productNumber' => $row['productNumber'],
        'name' => $row['name'],
        'stock' => (int) $row['stock'],
    ],
], $context);
```

Notes:
- `$productId ?? Uuid::randomHex()` gives new products an ID, so `upsert()` creates them and existing products are updated. The docs don't show this exact line. `Uuid` is `Shopware\Core\Framework\Uuid\Uuid`, which I didn't verify in the docs.
- The docs show `searchIds()` and `firstId()` but don't say what `firstId()` returns when nothing matches. Check for `null` before using it.
- For a new product, Shopware also requires fields such as a tax and a price. The docs I found don't cover those required fields, so check the product entity definition for your version.
- The `$context` argument comes from your command. The docs don't show how a console command obtains a `Context`.

If you need more detail on any step, such as required product fields or how to build the `Context` in a command, I can search for that.

Here's the full flow: create the event class, dispatch it from a service, and listen with a subscriber registered in `services.php`.

**1. Create the event class**

Implement `ShopwareSalesChannelEvent`, which gives you access to the `SalesChannelContext` [11][12]:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Core/Content/Example/Event/ExampleEvent.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Swag\BasicExample\Core\Content\Example\Event;

use Shopware\Core\Framework\Context;
use Shopware\Core\Framework\Event\ShopwareSalesChannelEvent;
use Shopware\Core\System\SalesChannel\SalesChannelContext;
use Swag\BasicExample\Core\Content\Example\ExampleEntity;

class ExampleEvent implements ShopwareSalesChannelEvent
{
    protected ExampleEntity $exampleEntity;

    protected SalesChannelContext $salesChannelContext;

    public function __construct(ExampleEntity $exampleEntity, SalesChannelContext $context)
    {
        $this->exampleEntity = $exampleEntity;
        $this->salesChannelContext = $context;
    }

    public function getExample(): ExampleEntity
    {
        return $this->exampleEntity;
    }

    public function getContext(): Context
    {
        return $this->salesChannelContext->getContext();
    }

    public function getSalesChannelContext(): SalesChannelContext
    {
        return $this->salesChannelContext;
    }
}
```
[12]

**2. Dispatch the event**

Inject the `EventDispatcherInterface` and call `dispatch` with the event object [1]:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Service/ExampleEventService.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Swag\BasicExample\Service;

use Shopware\Core\System\SalesChannel\SalesChannelContext;
use Swag\BasicExample\Core\Content\Example\Event\ExampleEvent;
use Swag\BasicExample\Core\Content\Example\ExampleEntity;
use Symfony\Contracts\EventDispatcher\EventDispatcherInterface;

class ExampleEventService
{
    private EventDispatcherInterface $eventDispatcher;

    public function __construct(EventDispatcherInterface $eventDispatcher)
    {
        $this->eventDispatcher = $eventDispatcher;
    }

    public function fireEvent(ExampleEntity $exampleEntity, SalesChannelContext $context)
    {
        $this->eventDispatcher->dispatch(new ExampleEvent($exampleEntity, $context));
    }
}
```
[1]

Call `fireEvent()` where your plugin finishes processing the example entity.

**3. Listen to the event**

Create a subscriber that implements `EventSubscriberInterface`, mapping the event class to a method [3]:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Subscriber/ExampleEventSubscriber.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Swag\BasicExample\Subscriber;

use Swag\BasicExample\Core\Content\Example\Event\ExampleEvent;
use Symfony\Component\EventDispatcher\EventSubscriberInterface;

class ExampleEventSubscriber implements EventSubscriberInterface
{
    public static function getSubscribedEvents(): array
    {
        // <event to listen to> => <method to execute>
        return [
            ExampleEvent::class => 'onExampleEvent',
        ];
    }

    public function onExampleEvent(ExampleEvent $event): void
    {
        $salesChannelContext = $event->getSalesChannelContext();
        // Do something with $event->getExample() and $salesChannelContext
    }
}
```

The subscriber example in the docs uses event name constants, such as `ProductEvents::PRODUCT_LOADED_EVENT` [3]. For a custom event, the event class name is used as the key. The documentation I found does not explicitly show this form, so check it against your setup. The subscriber's method receives the event object, as in the docs' example [3].

**4. Register the subscriber**

Add the `kernel.event_subscriber` tag in `services.php` [14]:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Resources/config/services.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

use Swag\BasicExample\Subscriber\ExampleEventSubscriber;
use Symfony\Component\DependencyInjection\Loader\Configurator\ContainerConfigurator;

return static function (ContainerConfigurator $configurator): void {
    $services = $configurator->services();

    $services->set(ExampleEventSubscriber::class)
        ->tag('kernel.event_subscriber');
};
```
[14]

The docs also note that a subscriber without this registration is never called, and Shopware gives no obvious error in that case [3]. The `ExampleEventService` also needs to be registered as a service in `services.php` so it can receive the `EventDispatcherInterface`. The docs I retrieved don't show that step, so I can't confirm the exact configuration for it.

To react to field changes, you need to request a changeset from Shopware's write process. To ship the delivery, you use the state machine. The docs cover both, but they don't show how to test a specific field inside the changeset, so that part is up to you.

**1. Detect changed fields on order updates**

Shopware doesn't add the changeset to the event by default. You have to request it in a `PreWriteValidationEvent` subscriber and read it in your order-written listener [1]:

```php
public static function getSubscribedEvents(): array
{
    return [
        PreWriteValidationEvent::class => 'triggerChangeSet',
        OrderEvents::ORDER_WRITTEN_EVENT => 'onOrderWritten',
    ];
}

public function triggerChangeSet(PreWriteValidationEvent $event): void
{
    if ($event->getContext()->getVersionId() !== Defaults::LIVE_VERSION) {
        return;
    }

    foreach ($event->getCommands() as $command) {
        if (!$command instanceof ChangeSetAware) {
            continue;
        }

        if ($command->getEntityName() !== OrderDefinition::ENTITY_NAME) {
            continue;
        }

        $command->requestChangeSet();
    }
}

public function onOrderWritten(EntityWrittenEvent $event): void
{
    if ($event->getContext()->getVersionId() !== Defaults::LIVE_VERSION) {
        return;
    }

    foreach ($event->getWriteResults() as $result) {
        $changeSet = $result->getChangeSet();

        // Do stuff
    }
}
```

[1] The docs don't say which methods the changeset object offers for checking a particular field, so check its class in the Shopware source. Insert commands can't produce a changeset because the whole entity is new [2].

**2. Set the delivery to shipped**

Inject `Shopware\Core\System\StateMachine\StateMachineRegistry` and use the `ship` transition on `order_delivery` [7][11]. Using the order's delivery ID:

```php
public function setOrderDeliveryToShipped(string $orderId, $context): void
{
    $criteria = new Criteria();
    $criteria->addFilter(new EqualsFilter('orderId', $orderId));

    $orderDeliveryEntityId = $this->orderDeliveryRepository->searchIds($criteria, $context)->firstId();

    $this->stateMachineRegistry->transition(new Transition(
        OrderDeliveryDefinition::ENTITY_NAME,
        $orderDeliveryEntityId,
        'ship',
        'stateId'
    ), $context);
}
```

[11] The `order_delivery.repository` service is injected via the dependency injection container [11]. Alternatively, you can load the order with the `deliveries` association and use the first delivery's ID [11].

Combining the two: in your order-written listener, when the relevant field is in the changeset, call `setOrderDeliveryToShipped()`. Be careful not to create a loop, for example by shipping the order inside a listener that is triggered by that same change.
